Step‑wise Explanation
Define Problem  
Find the length of the longest subsequence in a string that is a palindrome.

Recurrence Relation

If first and last characters match → L(i, j) = L(i+1, j-1) + 2

Else → L(i, j) = max(L(i+1, j), L(i, j-1))

Dynamic Programming Table

Create dp[n][n] where n = len(string)

Initialize diagonal as 1 (single characters are palindromes)

Fill Table

Iterate substring lengths from 2 to n

Update dp[i][j] using recurrence relation

Final Answer

Result is stored at dp[0][n-1]

Complexity

Time: O(n^2)

Space: O(n^2) (can be optimized to O(n))



Step‑wise Explanation
Define Recursive Function

Base case: single character → length = 1

If indices cross → length = 0

Recurrence Relation

If characters match → 2 + recurse(i+1, j-1)

Else → max(recurse(i+1, j), recurse(i, j-1))

Memoization

Use a dictionary or lru_cache to store results of subproblems.

Avoid recomputation → improves efficiency.

Final Answer

Call function with full string range (0, n-1).

Complexity

Time: O(n^2) with memoization

Space: O(n^2) due to recursion + cache



